id="patr-1" class="collapsed section-content patr-section" role="region"><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('ptc890556d').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Eternidad del Verbo</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="ptc890556d" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Agustín de Hipona</strong> (354-430) <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.1]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"In principio erat Verbum. This Word is not what we speak and passes away. This is the Word that remains. Consider how poor human speech is: my words strike your ears, and if you do not understand, I have spoken in vain. But the Word of God is always spoken and always heard."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Juan Crisóstomo</strong> (c.349-407) <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.1]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"The evangelist did not say 'In the beginning was made' or 'came to be' but 'was' (ἦν), showing the eternal pre-existence of the Word without beginning."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Hilario de Poitiers</strong> (c.310-367) <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.1]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Years, centuries, ages, are passed over, place what beginning thou wilt in thy imagining, thou graspest it not in time, for He, from Whom it is derived, still was."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Cirilo de Alejandría</strong> (c.376-444) <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.1]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"There is nothing older than 'the beginning' if we stay with the definition of beginning (for there cannot be a beginning of a beginning), or else it would diverge from being in truth a beginning if there is something else one can imagine before it or that arises before it."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los Padres afirman unánimemente la eternidad del Verbo, enfatizando que 'era' (ἦν) indica existencia eterna sin comienzo temporal. El 'principio' no denota límite temporal sino eternidad absoluta.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt2b5c9d78').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Divinidad del Verbo</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt2b5c9d78" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Atanasio de Alejandría</strong> (c.296-373) <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.1]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"If they were disputing concerning any human being, then let them exercise reason in this human way, both concerning his Word and his Son. But if their dispute concerns God, who created humanity, let them no longer entertain human thoughts but others that are above."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Hilario de Poitiers</strong> (c.310-367) <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.1]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"[The Son], being God, is nothing else than God. For when I hear the words 'And the Word was God,' they do not merely tell me that the Son was called God; they reveal to my understanding that he is God."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Ambrosio de Milán</strong> (c.340-397) <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.1]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"And 'the Word was God.' This Word, therefore, consists not in uttered speech but in the designation of celestial excellence, so that Photinus's teaching is refuted."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Cipriano</strong> (c.200-258) <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.1]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"That the same Christ is the Word of God. In the forty-fourth Psalm: 'My heart hath breathed out a good Word. I tell my works to the King.' Also in the thirty-second Psalm: 'By the Word of God were the heavens made fast; and all their strength by the breath of His mouth.'"</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los Padres defienden firmemente la plena divinidad del Verbo contra herejías arrianas y otras. El Verbo no es meramente llamado Dios sino que es Dios por naturaleza y esencia.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('ptb904f4a8').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Distinción de Personas en la Trinidad</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="ptb904f4a8" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Juan Crisóstomo</strong> (c.349-407) <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.1]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"And 'the Word was with God' shows distinct personhood, while 'the Word was God' shows unity of essence."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4
